  <title>Window appearance</title>

<p>The <gui>Appearance</gui> group of the preferences changes how the
  windows of <app>Pano Terminal</app> look:</p>
  <steps>
<item><p>Press the menu button in the top-right corner of the window and select <gui style="menuitem">Preferences</gui>.</p></item>
<item><p>Change the settings in the <gui>Appearance</gui> group. They apply at once.</p></item>
</steps>

  <terms>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Theme Variant</gui></title>
      <p>Follow the light or dark style of the system, or always use one of
      them.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Rounded corners</gui></title>
      <p>Keep the rounded corners of the windows, round only the top ones, or
      make all of them square.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Space Around the Text</gui></title>
      <p>Leave no space, a small space or a large space between the text and
      the edges of the terminal.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Color the Header Bar Like the Terminal</gui></title>
      <p>When the active terminal uses a <link xref="app-colors">color
      scheme</link> or colors of its own, give the header bar and the tabs
      its background and text colors, so that the window looks like one
      piece.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Highlight Root and Remote Sessions</gui></title>
      <p>While the active terminal runs a program as root, for example with
      <cmd>sudo</cmd> or <cmd>su</cmd>, the header bar turns red. While it is
      connected to another computer with <cmd>ssh</cmd> or <cmd>mosh</cmd>,
      the header bar turns purple. The tabs of such terminals get a warning or
      server icon.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Tab Bar Position</gui></title>
      <p>Show the tabs at the top, bottom, left or right of the window, see
      <link xref="gs-tabs#tab-position"/>.</p>
    </item>
  </terms>

  <p><gui>Dim Inactive Panes</gui> and <gui>Show Pane Titles</gui> are
  described in <link xref="gs-split-panes#look"/>.</p>

